WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.325
Lesson 0.2.

00:00:02.325 --> 00:00:05.000
Opening and securing an exchange account.

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:07.352
By the end of this lesson, you'll be able

00:00:07.352 --> 00:00:09.983
to open an account on a reputable exchange and

00:00:09.983 --> 00:00:12.055
lock it down before putting money in.

00:00:13.355 --> 00:00:14.645
Here's the plan.

00:00:14.645 --> 00:00:16.330
First, the key ideas.

00:00:16.330 --> 00:00:19.045
Then, a worked example, step by step.

00:00:19.045 --> 00:00:20.570
Then, your checklist.

00:00:20.570 --> 00:00:22.732
And finally, a three-question quiz.

00:00:24.032 --> 00:00:26.858
An exchange is a company that lets you swap

00:00:26.858 --> 00:00:30.627
your normal money (dollars, pounds, euros) for crypto.

00:00:30.627 --> 00:00:31.927
It's your on-ramp.

00:00:31.927 --> 00:00:36.757
Choose one that is: Well-known examples include Coinbase, Kraken,

00:00:36.757 --> 00:00:41.447
Binance, Bybit and OKX, but availability differs by country.

00:00:41.447 --> 00:00:44.037
Pick one that's legally available to you.

00:00:44.037 --> 00:00:46.370
You'll be asked for KYC ("know your

00:00:46.370 --> 00:00:49.652
customer"): ID and sometimes proof of address.

00:00:49.652 --> 00:00:54.054
This is normal and legally required for regulated exchanges.

00:00:54.054 --> 00:00:57.695
Secure it before depositing: First, Licensed or registered

00:00:57.695 --> 00:01:01.574
where you live (check your country's financial regulator).

00:01:01.574 --> 00:01:05.674
Next, Large and long-established, with a good security record.

00:01:05.674 --> 00:01:08.987
Then, Supports your bank's deposit method and

00:01:08.987 --> 00:01:12.300
the coins/networks you'll use (ETH, USDC, and

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:15.769
low-cost networks such as Arbitrum or Base).

00:01:15.769 --> 00:01:18.559
Also, Unique, long password stored in

00:01:18.559 --> 00:01:22.309
a password manager (e.g. Bitwarden, 1Password).

00:01:22.309 --> 00:01:24.994
On top of that, Secure your email first.

00:01:24.994 --> 00:01:26.823
Whoever controls your email can

00:01:26.823 --> 00:01:29.067
often reset your exchange account.

00:01:29.067 --> 00:01:31.482
Give it its own strong password and 2FA.

00:01:32.782 --> 00:01:37.622
And Two-factor authentication (2FA) with an authenticator app (e.g.

00:01:37.622 --> 00:01:41.907
Google Authenticator, Authy) or a hardware security key.

00:01:41.907 --> 00:01:44.297
Avoid SMS codes: phone numbers

00:01:44.297 --> 00:01:46.832
can be hijacked ("SIM swap").

00:01:46.832 --> 00:01:49.062
Plus, Save the 2FA backup codes

00:01:49.062 --> 00:01:51.732
offline (on paper, stored safely).

00:01:51.732 --> 00:01:54.245
One more: Turn on the anti-phishing code

00:01:54.245 --> 00:01:56.390
if offered (a word that appears in

00:01:56.390 --> 00:01:58.896
every real email from the exchange).

00:01:58.896 --> 00:02:02.347
And finally, Turn on the withdrawal address allowlist if

00:02:02.347 --> 00:02:06.221
offered (withdrawals can only go to addresses you pre-approved).

00:02:07.521 --> 00:02:10.036
Let's work an example, step by step.

00:02:10.036 --> 00:02:13.609
Maria signs up, completes KYC in 10 minutes, then

00:02:13.609 --> 00:02:18.682
spends another 10 on security: password manager, authenticator-app 2FA

00:02:18.682 --> 00:02:22.183
on both her email and the exchange, backup codes

00:02:22.183 --> 00:02:26.056
on paper, and an anti-phishing code "blue-kettle".

00:02:26.056 --> 00:02:28.743
Later, an email arrives about a "suspicious

00:02:28.743 --> 00:02:31.181
login" with no "blue-kettle" in it.

00:02:31.181 --> 00:02:33.031
She knows it's fake and deletes it.

00:02:34.331 --> 00:02:35.791
Here's your checklist.

00:02:35.791 --> 00:02:37.326
Do it now, for real.

00:02:37.326 --> 00:02:40.050
First, Exchange chosen: legally available

00:02:40.050 --> 00:02:42.101
and reputable where I live.

00:02:42.101 --> 00:02:44.036
Next, KYC complete.

00:02:44.036 --> 00:02:46.042
Then, Email secured with its

00:02:46.042 --> 00:02:48.141
own strong password + 2FA.

00:02:48.141 --> 00:02:51.605
Also, Exchange 2FA via authenticator app

00:02:51.605 --> 00:02:54.101
or security key (not SMS).

00:02:54.101 --> 00:02:56.806
On top of that, Backup codes stored offline.

00:02:58.106 --> 00:03:00.794
And finally, Anti-phishing code and withdrawal

00:03:00.794 --> 00:03:02.796
allowlist switched on (if offered).

00:03:04.096 --> 00:03:05.306
Question 1.

00:03:05.306 --> 00:03:07.591
Why secure your email before the exchange?

00:03:12.011 --> 00:03:15.451
The answer: Email is often how accounts are reset.

00:03:15.451 --> 00:03:18.376
Whoever controls it can take over the exchange account.

00:03:19.676 --> 00:03:20.921
Question 2.

00:03:20.921 --> 00:03:22.786
Why avoid SMS 2FA?

00:03:27.206 --> 00:03:30.397
The answer: Attackers can hijack phone numbers

00:03:30.397 --> 00:03:32.706
(SIM swap) and receive your codes.

00:03:34.006 --> 00:03:35.276
Question 3.

00:03:35.276 --> 00:03:37.001
What does a withdrawal allowlist do?

00:03:41.421 --> 00:03:43.581
The answer: Only lets withdrawals go

00:03:43.581 --> 00:03:45.741
to addresses you approved in advance.

00:03:47.041 --> 00:03:48.286
Let's recap.

00:03:48.286 --> 00:03:50.533
Licensed or registered where you live

00:03:50.533 --> 00:03:53.376
(check your country's financial regulator).

00:03:53.376 --> 00:03:56.946
Large and long-established, with a good security record.

00:03:56.946 --> 00:03:59.872
Supports your bank's deposit method and

00:03:59.872 --> 00:04:03.171
the coins/networks you'll use (ETH, USDC.

00:04:03.171 --> 00:04:05.553
Unique, long password stored in a

00:04:05.553 --> 00:04:08.706
password manager (e.g. Bitwarden, 1Password).

00:04:10.006 --> 00:04:12.521
Do the checklist now, before you move on.

00:04:12.521 --> 00:04:14.565
Next up, Lesson 0.3: Buying

00:04:14.565 --> 00:04:17.266
your first crypto without overpaying.
